speechtext:
I wish to support this item and to compliment the minister on increasing it. A short time ago I had occasion to go to the psychiatric hospital in Toronto near Queen's Park to see a friend who I had not known was sick. Apparently the party I have in mind had a mental breakdown. That was the first occasion on which I had to go to such a hospital, and while there I made it my business to inquire, as much as I could, into what the hospital was doing. I had conversations on other visits and learned that Doctor Clarence W. Hincks was the head of the committee on national mental hygiene, in Toronto, perhaps for all Canada. I arranged to get. in touch with him because I was anxious to learn as much as I could about the programme of mental hygiene. I had a conference with Doctor Hincks and made a short memorandum consisting of a number of his statements. I shall read four of them. They are as follows:
(a)	That one out of every ten persons examined for the armed services was mentally affected or afflicted.
(b)	That thirty-five per cent of illness is attributed to mental disorder of some kind;
(c)	That there are only ten mental clinics in Canada to take care of the problem;
(d)	That the services to combat the problem are inadequate.
He did not blame anybody for that. I gathered that this is more or less a new conception. I gathered that only in the last two or three years has a department really made an effort to deal with the problem.
